in Hong Kong this morning police fired  tear gas to break up what is the 13th  weekend of pro-democracy demonstrations  protesters were also hit with blue dyed  water when police used a water cannon to  try to push them away from the  barricaded zone authorities say some  protesters responded and threw gasoline  bombs toward police lines the violent  clash is happening near the Chinese  government's main office in the city  Remy innocencio is in Hong Kong  thousands of protesters defied a ban to  March today what they're doing is  illegal but it seems that their anger  against their government Trump's their  fear of getting arrested that anger has  only grown in the past 48 hours  police arrested nine pro-democracy  legislators and leading activists to  take them off the streets and to  intimidate the entire movement that  includes Joshua Wong one of the student  leaders of the 2014 umbrella revolution  he is now out on bail today is also the  5th anniversary of when Beijing rejected  Hong Kong's call for universal suffrage  Hongkongers don't have the right to  directly elect their chief executive  Carrie Lam is now polling at record low  approvals but they can't vote her out  and so they keep taking to the streets  this Sunday marks the start of the  fourth month of protests this weekend  will continue to be unpredictable there  are already calls to block the roads  leading to the airport there are also  calls for students to boycott their  classes which start next week Raymond  Ascencio CBS News Hong Kong
 

